  "content": "Humla receives large number of international tourists\nRastriya Samachar Samiti (RSS)\nSunday, October 12, 2025\n0\nComments\nSimkot, October 12\nThe district of Humla received a large number of Indian pilgrims visiting Kailash, a holy religious shrine for Hindu devotees. Generally, Indian tourists make their journey to Kailash and Mansarovar in China’s Tibet through Hilsa in Humla.\nThis year’s season for Kailash journey for pilgrims is over with the dip in temperature. Hotelier Mim Lama said that the hotels in the Hilsa area made good income in this season, thanks to the rising number of tourists here. Each hotel earned more than a million Nepali rupees, Lama shared.\nNearly, 6,500 foreign tourists arrived in Humla district since the Hilsa border opened in May. DSP of Humla Shankar Pokharel said that almost 6,000 Indian pilgrims travelled through the district from Nepalgunj to visit Kailash.\n“Tourists from other countries too have arrived here,” DSP Pokharel said. This route is comparatively shorter for Indians and other nationals to visit Kailash Mansarovar. Officials shared that the number of pilgrims to use this route has increased sharply after the end of COVID-19.\nThe Simkot and Namkha rural municipalities also charge levy on foreigners for their entry in the district. Officials shared that an Indian should pay Rs 1,100 and other nationals should pay Rs 1,500 for their entry into the district.\nReact to this post\nHot Topics\ngold\nnepali cricket\nflood\nSushila Karki\ninsurance\nministers\nICC T20 World Cup Qualifier\nRastriya Samachar Samiti (RSS)\nRSS is a government-run national news portal of Nepal.\nMore From the Author\nConversation\nNew\nOld\nPopular\nAdd Comment\nLogin to comment\nRemenber me\nForgot password?\nLogin\nNot have account yet?\nSignup Now\nOr use Social Media?\nGoogle\nFacebook\nTwitter\nForgot Password?\nSend Password Reset Link\nBack\nRelated News\n4 ministers set to join cabinet; oath-taking likely by Monday as indigenous collective condemns racist attacks on Tashi Lhazom\n33 minutes ago\nFlower farmers complain of unfair prices, urge government to fix rates\n3 hours ago\nOne killed in motorcycle accident in Kapan\n3 hours ago\nFear among Raute community after drunk intruders enter settlement; security demanded\n3 hours ago\nNepal to face UAE in Super Six clash of ICC T20 World Cup Qualifier\n3 hours ago\nInsurance claims totaling Rs 23.22 billion registered so far; payments reach Rs 1.58 billion\n3 hours ago\nMost Read\nUAE introduces new rules for Visit Visa Sponsorship\n12 stranded backpackers rescued from Langtang\nWhen a joke fuels division: Casual hate speech and the Gen Z revolution in Nepal\nGold price reaches record high at Rs 235,960 per tola\nGold price surges by Rs 10,000 per tola after Dashain\nSubscribe to our newsletter\nSubscribe to Onlinekhabar English to get notified of exclusive news stories.\nEditor's Pick\nEditor's Pick\n4 government buildings, including Supreme Court, declared unfit for use\n3 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nCharred body found at Koteshwor Bhatbhateni, Gen-Z protest death toll rises to 74\n4 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nNo consensus on Hami Nepal’s claim of leading the Gen-Z movement\n4 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nDecades-old irrigation projects stall as Madhesh faces drought, food crisis looms\n3 months ago\nEditor's Pick\nNepal’s farming crisis: When plans stay on paper and fields suffer\n3 months ago\nAlso Read\nSociety\nFear among Raute community after drunk intruders enter settlement; security demanded\n3 hours ago\nCricket\nNepal to face UAE in Super Six clash of ICC T20 World Cup Qualifier\n3 hours ago\nOpinion\nThe rise of Nepal’s first woman Prime Minister\n7 hours ago\nPolitics\nWho do student unions stand for? Questions rise after Gen Z Movement\n2 days ago\nEconomy\nThai AirAsia to resume flights from Gautam Buddha International Airport\n2 days ago",
